<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Hardened-runtime entitlements for Tangent.app. See build/macos/README.md
      for why each key is here and what breaks without it.

      FORMAT WARNING, learned the hard way: AMFI parses this file with its own
      strict XML reader, not CFPreferences. The DOCTYPE line above is REQUIRED
      and comments must live INSIDE the dict — a comment between the <?xml?>
      declaration and <plist> makes codesign fail with "Failed to parse
      entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error", which names a line number
      but not the actual rule. Verified by bisection, not guessed.
    -->

    <!--
      CAPTURE. Apple uses the same key names for App Sandbox and Hardened
      Runtime, so these are correct for a non-sandboxed notarized app too. Both
      are in the Developer-ID-allowed set: no provisioning profile, no App ID
      registration, no entitlement request, and the notary service accepts them.

      These gate the DEVICE. NSCameraUsageDescription and
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist gate the PROMPT. Both are
      required and they fail DIFFERENTLY: a missing entitlement is a silent
      refusal with no Privacy row ever appearing, while a missing usage string
      terminates the process.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      PLUGIN HOSTING. Library validation restricts loading to code signed by the
      same Team ID or by Apple, which every third-party VST3 fails by
      definition — dlopen returns "different Team IDs". This is the entitlement
      Apple provides for hosts, and it is why they warn against enabling it
      unless you genuinely load third-party plug-ins. Tangent genuinely does.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Disabling library validation is NOT sufficient alone, and the failure is
      easy to misdiagnose because the plugin loads successfully and then dies
      inside its own allocator. Verified by experiment (arm64, Darwin 25.6),
      hardened host dlopen'ing a foreign-signed dylib:

        runtime, no entitlements             -> dlopen fails, "different Team IDs"
        runtime + disable-library-validation -> loads, then MAP_JIT fails, EINVAL
        runtime + both of the above          -> works

      Almost every serious instrument JITs its DSP graph, so this is the normal
      case. Signing the PLUGIN with allow-jit does nothing: a dylib carries no
      entitlements of its own and an in-process plugin runs under the host's.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      AND allow-jit is not sufficient either, which cost the owner a build and
      an app that would not reopen.

      `allow-jit` grants MAP_JIT — the modern, Apple-sanctioned way to make a
      page executable, and what a plugin that builds its DSP graph at runtime
      uses. A plugin that instead loads a Mach-O image it has assembled IN
      MEMORY does not go near MAP_JIT: it goes through
      `NSCreateObjectFileImageFromMemory`, and the kernel refuses to run
      initializers off pages no signature covers.

      The refusal is not an error return. It is SIGKILL, delivered to the HOST,
      with `CODESIGNING / Invalid Page` and no unwind — so there is nothing to
      catch, nothing logged on our side, and the app simply vanishes the
      instant the plugin is chosen. Loading Rhodes Anthology did exactly this,
      and because the choice is persisted the next launch reloaded it and died
      again: an app that could not be reopened at all.

        runtime + library-validation + allow-jit  -> SIGKILL on such a plugin
        runtime + the three below                 -> loads

      **This is a real relaxation and it is chosen deliberately.** It stops the
      kernel validating executable pages in this process, so a hostile plugin
      has an easier time here than it would elsewhere. Every serious macOS host
      ships it for this reason; the honest alternative is not "leave it off",
      it is out-of-process hosting, where a plugin cannot take the app down at
      all. That is worth doing and is not this.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
